<PubmedArticleSet><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">34373451</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2021</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>23</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>03</Month><Day>07</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2041-1723</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>12</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2021</Year><Month>Aug</Month><Day>09</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Nature communications</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Nat Commun</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Integrative oncogene-dependency mapping identifies RIT1 vulnerabilities and synergies in lung cancer.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>4789</StartPage><MedlinePgn>4789</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">4789</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s41467-021-24841-y</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>CRISPR-based cancer dependency maps are accelerating advances in cancer precision medicine, but adequate functional maps are limited to the most common oncogenes. To identify opportunities for therapeutic intervention in other rarer subsets of cancer, we investigate the oncogene-specific dependencies conferred by the lung cancer oncogene, RIT1. Here, genome-wide CRISPR screening in KRAS, EGFR, and RIT1-mutant isogenic lung cancer cells identifies shared and unique vulnerabilities of each oncogene. Combining this genetic data with small-molecule sensitivity profiling, we identify a unique vulnerability of RIT1-mutant cells to loss of spindle assembly checkpoint regulators. Oncogenic RIT1<sup>M90I</sup> weakens the spindle assembly checkpoint and perturbs mitotic timing, resulting in sensitivity to Aurora A inhibition. In addition, we observe synergy between mutant RIT1 and activation of YAP1 in multiple models and frequent nuclear overexpression of YAP1 in human primary RIT1-mutant lung tumors. These results provide a genome-wide atlas of oncogenic RIT1 functional interactions and identify components of the RAS pathway, spindle assembly checkpoint, and Hippo/YAP1 network as candidate therapeutic targets in RIT1-mutant lung cancer.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#169; 2021. The Author(s).</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Vichas</LastName><ForeName>Athea</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Human Biology Division, Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center, Seattle, WA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Riley</LastName><ForeName>Amanda K</ForeName><Initials>AK</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-5720-3269</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Human Biology Division, Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center, Seattle, WA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Molecular and Cellular Biology Program, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Nkinsi</LastName><ForeName>Naomi T</ForeName><Initials>NT</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Human Biology Division, Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center, Seattle, WA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kamlapurkar</LastName><ForeName>Shriya</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Human Biology Division, Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center, Seattle, WA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Parrish</LastName><ForeName>Phoebe C R</ForeName><Initials>PCR</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-3530-0105</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Human Biology Division, Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center, Seattle, WA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Genome Sciences, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lo</LastName><ForeName>April</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Human Biology Division, Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center, Seattle, WA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Genome Sciences, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Duke</LastName><ForeName>Fujiko</ForeName><Initials>F</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Broad Institute of MIT &amp; Harvard, Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Jennifer</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Broad Institute of MIT &amp; Harvard, Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Fung</LastName><ForeName>Iris</ForeName><Initials>I</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Broad Institute of MIT &amp; Harvard, Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Watson</LastName><ForeName>Jacqueline</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Broad Institute of MIT &amp; Harvard, Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Rees</LastName><ForeName>Matthew</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-2987-7581</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Broad Institute of MIT &amp; Harvard, Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gabel</LastName><ForeName>Austin M</ForeName><Initials>AM</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-1418-0327</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Genome Sciences, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Medical Scientist Training Program, University of Washington, Seattle, W